  "articleBody": "Embarking on a disability remodel can feel overwhelming, but being aware of common missteps can save you a lot of headache and ensure a better outcome. One frequent mistake we see is **skipping a detailed written bid and timeline.** Without it, you're flying blind on costs and project duration. Always insist on a clear, comprehensive proposal that spells everything out. Another pitfall is **failing to check references and similar past work.** An accessibility remodel isn't just any renovation; it requires specific expertise. Make sure your contractor can demonstrate experience with projects like yours. Then there's the mistake of **choosing a contractor without relevant accessibility experience or proper licensing/insurance.** This is critical for both safety and quality. Always verify their credentials. It's also easy to **underestimate turning space for wheelchairs** or other mobility aids, especially in bathrooms and kitchens. Good design considers these clearances meticulously. And don't forget **slippery or steep exterior routes.** Ramps and pathways need to be safe in all weather. Finally, many folks **don't plan for future needs**, designing only for today. A well-thought-out remodel considers how mobility might change over time, making adjustments that are durable and adaptable. Taking a little extra time upfront to avoid these issues will pay off immensely in the long run.",
